/// An argument may name the parameter it is for, with '=>'.
///
/// '=>' scans today as '=' then '>', so a named call fails with
/// 'Expect expression!'
///
/// ⚠️ '=' cannot be the marker.  'H (Index = 1)' already PARSES today -- as a
/// call passing the comparison 'Index = 1' -- and fails only because Index is
/// not in scope at the call site.  Using '=' would silently change what an
/// existing program means; '=>' is unused.
///
/// Named arguments may come in any order, and any parameter neither supplied
/// positionally nor by name takes its default.  Positional arguments come
/// first: after the first named argument every remaining one must be named.
///
/// Fails with: Expect ')' after parameters.  (file rejected -- no test ran)
///
/// The signature's defaults are refused before the call's '=>' is ever reached,
/// so the recorded message is the one from the parameter list.
///
function Slice (Text : String, Start : Integer := 0, Count : Integer := 1) : String;
begin
    Exit Copy (Text, Start, Count);
end

test 'An argument may name its parameter';
begin
    AssertEqual (Str ('ell'), Slice (Str ('hello'), Count => 3, Start => 1));
    AssertEqual (Str ('he'),  Slice (Text => Str ('hello'), Count => 2));
    AssertEqual (Str ('h'),   Slice (Text => Str ('hello')));
end
